# Annotations

Use [Material annotations](https://squidfunk.github.io/mkdocs-material/reference/annotations/) for a short note that opens from a numbered marker. `attr_list`, `md_in_html`, and `pymdownx.superfences` are enabled. Do not change the annotation icon.

A marker is `(1)` in the text. The matching note is a numbered list immediately after the block. The `{ .annotate }` class goes on the outermost block only.

```markdown
A bare number is a width in millimetres. (1)
{ .annotate }

1.  A value like `"50%"` is a percentage of the available width.
```

- One list defines every marker in that block. The numbers start at `1`.
- In an admonition, add `annotate` after the type: `!!! note annotate "Title"`. The list follows the admonition, not the indented body.
- Nest a note by putting `{ .annotate }` on that list item and a new list beneath it.
- Do not put a marker in a table. Annotations do not position correctly there.
- In a content tab, put `{ .annotate }` on the tab's block, not on the `===` line. Keep the numbered list inside that tab.
- A code comment such as `# (1)` is a code annotation. Use that syntax inside a fence, not `{ .annotate }`.
- Prefer an admonition when the reader must see the note without clicking.
